{"answer":"The supplied results do not specifically address “transformer recommender popularity bias” (i.e., popularity bias in recommendation models). They instead discuss how transformers/LLMs and related generation or writing processes can shift measured style signals, which is adjacent but not the same topic. [1,3,5,8,10,9]

Several papers show that rewriting or personalization can homogenize or alter linguistic/stylometric signals rather than preserve original identity: LLM rewriting produces directional “stylometric normalization” in narrative style features. [1] Post-editing can increase similarity to a person’s unassisted writing but still leaves detectable LLM stylistic traces and reduced stylistic diversity. [3] Differentially-private text rewriting converges toward a “non-involved and non-persuasive register,” homogenizing nuanced markers. [8] Federated personalized generation can average out author-specific signals (author-style homogenization), and a proposed method (FAVoR) aims to retain authorial voice. [10] PersonalBench finds inference-time personalization modulates style but does not bridge the human-LLM authorship gap. [9]

If you meant something like “bias toward popular/well-modeled styles” in generative transformers, tell me how you define popularity bias; none of these papers directly evaluates popularity effects in recommendation.","grounded":false}
